Use case: stylized-concept. Asset type: production transparent 2D game sprite attack sheet.
Generate EXACTLY 24 complete full-body right-facing Reaper poses, strict 6 columns x 4 rows equal-cell grid, left-to-right top-to-bottom, wide 3:2 canvas ideally 3072x2048, transparent background. Full body AND entire weapon must be inside each cell with invisible gutters. No text, numbers, grid, scenery, shadows, duplicates, speed lines, glows or painted slash effects.
Input images are identity/style references. Preserve the approved Reaper's exact ivory skull and single cyan eye, dark hood, bronze armor/boots, black robe with burgundy torn cloak, and ornate rigid scythe with skull socket and ONE curved pointed cutting blade. Match the hand-painted gothic storybook artwork. The old attack reference is ONLY for character/weapon identity; replace its short awkward sweep entirely.
This is one continuous powerful LOWER FORWARD REAPING CUT, then recovery. Every pose must be distinct with progressive torso/hip rotation, two-handed grip and flexible knees. The sharp POINT must lead the cutting motion from the beginning of the forward stroke to its finish; the curved cutting edge follows the point and the shaft/ornate blunt skull socket trails it. Do not have the blunt back of the weapon enter the cut first. Do not flip or morph blade anatomy. A clear long arc sweeps from upper front around chest height, THROUGH the space ahead of the body at waist height, and down toward shin height, reaching well beyond the front boot. Not a short overhead chop. Weapon remains the same size in all cells. Both hands stay attached to shaft.
Keep character pelvis at 42% cell width, boot baseline at 90%, face around 55% width, leave generous right-hand space for the scythe reaching 95% width. Character skull/torso size constant; full figure including carried weapon at most 88% cell height.
FRAME PLAN:
1–4 ready-to-windup: running stance settles, second hand grasps shaft, scythe rotates back and up as shoulders coil;
5–6 initiate forward cut, pointed edge rotates ahead into the upper front reach;
7–9 point leads outward toward right at shoulder/chest height;
10–12 blade point traces a smooth forward arc down through chest to waist;
13–15 tip leads at maximum forward reach, hips/shoulders rotate together, blade crosses low waist;
16–18 tip sweeps downward through thigh to shin height, long follow-through with knees flexed;
19–20 cut slows after the low sweep, cloak follows the turn;
21–24 controlled recovery lifting the scythe back behind shoulder, weight shifts into running, ending close to the start's normal run silhouette.
All frames anatomically consistent. Actual full-body postures and curved blade orientation visibly progress. A polished game animation sheet, not 24 copies of an idle character.
